        "filled_by": "remotion:ClaudeComposerAsk",
        "at": "2026-10-18T21:29:09"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B01",
      "act": "BLUF",
      "narration_text": "Here's the tempting conclusion. Self-supervised pretraining helps on Raman spectra, so surely it's proven for SERS too. Not yet. On SERS, it's still untested.",
      "new_visual_element": "proven → untested",
      "shot": {
        "type": "REMOTION",
        "remotion": {
          "pattern": "BrutalistHesitantWriter",
          "props": {
            "text": "Pretraining\nhelps.\nOn SERS:\nproven.",
            "triggerWords": "proven",
            "replacementWords": "untested",
            "face": "mono",
            "fontSize": 225,
            "lineSpacing": 0.95,
            "seed": "sers45-bluf-proven",
            "lead_silence_s": 0.4,
            "mistakeRate": 3,
            "hesitateWithin": 1,
            "hesitateBetween": 5,
            "charMs": 30
          },
          "rendered": {
            "out": "media/B01.mp4",
            "at": "2026-10-19T01:13:25.798016+00:00"
          }
        }
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B01.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 9.15,
      "render_duration_s": 9.166666666666666,
      "build": {
        "status": "VIDEO",
        "src": "media/B01.mp4",
        "filled_by": "remotion:BrutalistHesitantWriter",
        "at": "2026-10-18T21:29:09"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B02",
      "act": "WHICH_BOTTLENECK",
      "narration_text": "The revision opens with a question the draft never asked: what's actually scarce? If you have too few spectra overall, augmentation is the first move. If you have plenty of spectra but too few labels, self-supervised pretraining is the better fit.",
      "new_visual_element": "Spectra vs labels fork",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "own",
        "lane": "manim",
        "show": [
          {
            "at": 0.0,
            "event": "title + footer"
          },
          {
            "at": 0.8,
            "event": "question: what is scarce?"
          },
          {
            "at": 3.3,
            "event": "too few spectra → augmentation"
          },
          {
            "at": 6.1,
            "event": "too few labels → pretraining"
          },
          {
            "at": 9.4,
            "event": "kicker"
          }
        ]
      },
      "graphic": {
        "engine": "manim",
        "manim": "B02_WhichBottleneck",
        "production_viz": {
          "label": "Which bottleneck?",
          "mechanic": "Question card forks into two answer cards",
          "note": "Paraphrases the revised opening paragraph; no figures."
        }
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B02.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 12.93,
      "render_duration_s": 12.958333333333334,
      "build": {
        "status": "MANIM",
        "src": "manim/B02.mp4",
        "filled_by": "manim",
        "at": "2026-10-18T21:29:09"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B03",
      "act": "AUGMENT",
      "narration_text": "Augmentation is the cheap first step, because it needs no new data, only transformations of spectra you already have. But it's only as good as its realism. Add far more noise than your instrument produces, and the model learns to survive conditions it will never see. So the rule is: set augmentation from measured instrument and substrate noise.",
      "new_visual_element": "Realistic vs excessive noise",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "own",
        "lane": "manim",
        "show": [
          {
            "at": 0.0,
            "event": "title + footer"
          },
          {
            "at": 1.3,
            "event": "illustrative spectrum draws"
          },
          {
            "at": 3.3,
            "event": "realistic noise band"
          },
          {
            "at": 7.6,
            "event": "band swells: excessive noise"
          },
          {
            "at": 11.8,
            "event": "band back; rule kicker"
          }
        ]
      },
      "graphic": {
        "engine": "manim",
        "manim": "B03_AugmentRealism",
        "production_viz": {
          "label": "Augment realistically",
          "mechanic": "Noise band around a spectrum grows past realism, then returns",
          "note": "Spectrum and noise bands are ILLUSTRATIVE (synthetic), labeled on screen. Rule paraphrases the revised §4.5."
        }
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B03.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 18.33,
      "render_duration_s": 18.333333333333332,
      "build": {
        "status": "MANIM",
        "src": "manim/B03.mp4",
        "filled_by": "manim",
        "at": "2026-10-18T21:29:09"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B04",
      "act": "COST",
      "narration_text": "Self-supervised learning is chosen for a different reason: the economics of SERS. Collecting raw spectra is cheap. Confirming what's in each one usually needs an independent reference method, and that's expensive. So pretrain on the big unlabeled pool, then fine-tune on the few spectra you could afford to label.",
      "new_visual_element": "Cheap spectra, expensive labels",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "own",
        "lane": "manim",
        "show": [
          {
            "at": 0.0,
            "event": "title + footer"
          },
          {
            "at": 0.8,
            "event": "big unlabeled pool (cheap)"
          },
          {
            "at": 4.3,
            "event": "three labeled spectra (expensive)"
          },
          {
            "at": 7.9,
            "event": "pretrain → fine-tune card"
          },
          {
            "at": 11.4,
            "event": "kicker"
          }
        ]
      },
      "graphic": {
        "engine": "manim",
        "manim": "B04_CostAsymmetry",
        "production_viz": {
          "label": "The cost asymmetry",
          "mechanic": "Many grey dots vs three terracotta dots, joined by a process card",
          "note": "Dot counts are schematic, not data. Cost reasoning paraphrases the revised §4.5."
        }
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B04.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 16.58,
      "render_duration_s": 16.583333333333332,
      "build": {
        "status": "MANIM",
        "src": "manim/B04.mp4",
        "filled_by": "manim",
        "at": "2026-10-18T21:29:09"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B05",
      "act": "EVIDENCE",
      "narration_text": "The draft just called this a gap. The revision cites two recent studies. Ren and colleagues' masked autoencoder reached eighty-three point nine percent after fine-tuning on limited labels, level with a fully supervised ResNet at eighty-three point four. Sun and Wang's SemiRaman reached eighty-nine point two and ninety-two percent, using only five and ten percent labeled data. The catch: both used conventional Raman spectra of bacteria, not SERS.",
      "new_visual_element": "SMAE + SemiRaman results",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "own",
        "lane": "manim",
        "show": [
          {
            "at": 0.0,
            "event": "title + footer"
          },
          {
            "at": 0.8,
            "event": "SMAE 83.90% vs ResNet 83.40%"
          },
          {
            "at": 8.0,
            "event": "SemiRaman 89.2% @5%, 92.0% @10% labels"
          },
          {
            "at": 14.7,
            "event": "bars dim; not-SERS caveat"
          }
        ]
      },
      "graphic": {
        "engine": "manim",
        "manim": "B05_Evidence",
        "production_viz": {
          "label": "The evidence",
          "mechanic": "Two bar panels, then all bars dim under the caveat",
          "note": "Ren et al. 2025 (arXiv:2504.16130; ESWA 292:128576): SMAE 83.90% vs supervised ResNet 83.40%. Sun & Wang 2026 (SAA 349:127356): 89.2% / 92.0% at 5% / 10% labels, checked by author against the paper. Both conventional Raman, bacteria."
        }
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B05.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 22.04,
      "render_duration_s": 22.041666666666668,
      "build": {
        "status": "MANIM",
        "src": "manim/B05.mp4",
        "filled_by": "manim",
        "at": "2026-10-18T21:29:09"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B06",
      "act": "VERDICT",
      "narration_text": "Here's the verdict. Section four point five now matches each strategy to its bottleneck: augmentation when spectra are scarce, pretraining when labels are. And it's honest about the evidence. Self-supervised pretraining is promising and low-risk to test on SERS, but until someone demonstrates it on SERS data, it isn't validated there.",
      "new_visual_element": "Verdict card",
      "shot": {
        "type": "REMOTION",
        "remotion": {
          "pattern": "ClaudeVerdictArtifact",
          "props": {
            "artifactTitle": "Section 4.5 · Revision",
            "artifactHeading": "Match the strategy to the shortage",
            "artifactLines": [
              "New framing: choose the strategy by the bottleneck, spectra or labels.",
              "Augmentation recommended first: no new data collection needed.",
              "Augmentation rule: set parameters from measured instrument and substrate noise.",
              "Self-supervised learning tied to SERS's cost asymmetry: cheap spectra, expensive labels.",
              "Uncited gap claim replaced with SMAE and SemiRaman results.",
              "Caveat added: both studies use conventional Raman, not SERS."
            ]
          },
          "rendered": {
            "out": "media/B06.mp4",
            "at": "2026-10-19T01:14:42.583184+00:00"
          }
        }
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B06.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 17.83,
